# mm.sdcard

An SD card in SPI mode, over portable mm.mcu SPI and one chip-select GPIO,
presented as an mm.fs BlockDevice of 512-byte blocks: what mm.fs.fat mounts
on a board's TF socket. It contains no vendor header and no board pin number;
a board's provider, through mm.sdcard.socket, or a program supplies the
wiring.

It follows the SD Association's Physical Layer Simplified Specification for
SPI mode: at 400 kHz, at least 74 clocks with chip select high, CMD0 into SPI
mode, CMD8 to tell a version 2 card from a version 1, ACMD41 until the card
leaves idle, CMD58 for the capacity class, CMD59 to turn CRC checking on,
CMD16 for 512-byte blocks on a standard-capacity card, and CMD9 for the CSD
the capacity is read from; then the data clock. Reads are CMD17 or CMD18 with
CMD12, writes CMD24 or CMD25 with the stop token and CMD13 after; every
command carries its CRC7, every data block its CRC16, and a block whose CRC
does not match is TransportError.

The card initialises on first use, not at construction, so a static card
object costs nothing on a board without one. Any transport failure forgets
the card, so the next call starts again from CMD0: a card pulled and
reinserted, or swapped for another, is found again. SDSC cards are
byte-addressed and SDHC and SDXC cards block-addressed; geometry hides the
difference. The SPI bus may be shared; the chip select is the only line this
module owns, and it is high between operations.
